3. CASE TEXT AND GROUNDING -- AND WHAT "ARP POLLING" ACTUALLY IS
------------------------------------------------------------------
AWPTCM T33246 "IPv4_ARP - ARP Polling".  STEP TEXT: NONE EXISTS (no exact-title match in
ck.db; only T43849 has step text).

**FINDING-A, carried from triage and CONFIRMED on the DUT in this run.**  TPS row 18 "ARP
Polling" is Project 2561, "ARP-polling for Web Auth"
(wiki.atlnz.lc ARP-polling_for_Web_Auth_TFS).  It is NOT a generic ARP-cache refresh feature:
the device sends DIRECTED ARP requests to an already web-authenticated supplicant and
deauthenticates it after `failcount` unanswered requests.  There is no `arp polling` anywhere
under `arp ?`.

The TFS's MRS text proposes `auth-web-server [arp-poll | ping-poll] enable`.  **That syntax does
not exist on this build**, re-confirmed here by read-only probe:

  awplus(config)# auth-web-server arp-poll ?
  % Unrecognized command

The shipped syntax, read off the DUT:

  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll ?
    enable                Enable ping polling
    failcount             Set the number of pings that is unanswered (default 5)
    interval              Set ping polling interval (default 30 sec)
    reauth-timer-refresh  Enable Reauthperiod updating when the response from supplicant
                          is received
    timeout               Set response waiting time (default 1 sec)
    type                  Ping polling type
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type ?
    arp   Enable polling via ARP
    ping  Enable polling via ICMP
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll interval ?   <1-65535>  Seconds
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll failcount ?  <1-100>  Count

TFS revision 0.3 (28/Apr/2021) is logged as "Change configuration syntax", so the shipped CLI is
the intended one.  **This is a documentation/TFS divergence, NOT a product defect, and the case
is NOT graded UNSUPPORTED for it** -- exactly as the dispatch directed.  Anyone grading T33246
against the TFS text verbatim would wrongly call the feature absent.

DEFAULTS, confirmed twice -- from the CLI help above and independently from the running state
before anything was configured:

  awplus# show auth-web-server
    PingPolling: disabled
    PingPollingType: Ping
    PingInterval: 30
    Timeout: 1
    FailCount: 5
    ReauthTimerRefresh: disabled

Also grounded in: TPS row 18 (priority 1, "Yes", note column empty); STANDING-ORDERS Section 7;
memory `satisfactory-level-of-testing`.  NOTE: `ip_feature_overview_guide.pdf` contains no
mention of "polling" -- the grounding for this case is the Project 2561 TFS, not that guide.

OBJECTIVE: with a web-authenticated supplicant present, `auth-web-server ping-poll type arp` +
`enable` makes the DUT send DIRECTED (unicast) ARP requests to that supplicant at `interval`;
`type ping` sends ICMP instead; a supplicant that stops answering is deauthenticated after
`failcount` unanswered polls; the configuration is removable and survives a reboot.


4. BENCH SETUP FOR THIS CASE
----------------------------
This case needs a real HTTP client that can reach the DUT's web-auth server.  To get one with
**no host networking change**, the DUT's vlan10 was re-addressed into the box's EXISTING eth3
subnet instead of giving the box an address in a test subnet:

  interface vlan10   10.38.22.34/27    (was 192.168.10.1/24 for cases 1-5)
  box eth3           10.38.22.33/27    unchanged, as the box has always had it

10.38.22.34 WAS CHECKED FREE BEFORE USE, and that check is itself worth recording.  `ip neigh`
initially showed `10.38.22.34 lladdr e0:1a:ea:d4:72:cb`, which looks like a live device.  It was
a STALE cache entry (memory `tb-host-stale-arp-after-recable`).  Proven by putting port1.0.10
back into VLAN 1, flushing the neighbour cache and ARP-sweeping the whole /27:

  sudo ip neigh flush dev eth3 ; for i in $(seq 33 62); do ping -c1 -W1 10.38.22.$i & done
  => of .33-.62, exactly ONE address answered:
     10.38.22.41 lladdr 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 REACHABLE
     28 others INCOMPLETE/FAILED, including .34 and .49 which had stale lladdrs before the flush.
  So 10.38.22.34 is free, and 10.38.22.41 is a real third party -- see section 9.

