6. STEPS
--------

STEP 1 -- BASELINE, POLLING DISABLED.  A web-authenticated supplicant is present and the DUT
          must not poll it.

  awplus# show auth-web-server -> PingPolling: disabled (the default)
  tool: sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L0-poll-off --out /tmp/ck1747/T33246 \
          --iface eth3 --sniff-only --wait 40
  cap L0-poll-off-eth3.pcap: **0 frame(s)**

  Reading: forty seconds, an authenticated supplicant on the port, and the segment is COMPLETELY
  silent -- not one frame of any kind.  That is the cleanest possible baseline: anything counted
  in the next leg is new.
  STEP 1 VERDICT: PASS (baseline established, default confirmed disabled).

STEP 2 -- `ping-poll type arp` + `enable`: DIRECTED ARP REQUESTS AT THE CONFIGURED INTERVAL.
          This is the TFS's core requirement -- the poll must be DIRECTED to the supplicant,
          not broadcast.

  awplus# configure terminal
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type arp
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll interval 10
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll failcount 3
  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll enable
  awplus(config)# end
  awplus# show auth-web-server
    PingPolling: enabled
    PingPollingType: ARP
    PingInterval: 10
    Timeout: 1
    FailCount: 3

  tool: sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L1-arp-poll --out /tmp/ck1747/T33246 \
          --iface eth3 --sniff-only --wait 45
  cap L1-arp-poll-eth3.pcap: 10 frame(s)
    x5  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34
                                       [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
    x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.33 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
                                       [eth 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

  Reading, three separate requirements met in one capture:
    - COUNT AND CADENCE: 5 polls in 45 s with `interval 10`.  The rate matches the configured
      interval, so the frames are the poll and not something incidental.
    - **DIRECTED, NOT BROADCAST**: the Ethernet destination of every poll is
      `00:0d:b9:55:77:0e`, the supplicant's own MAC -- NOT `ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff`.  Compare any
      ordinary ARP resolution in this group (T33248, T33247, T43848, T43849), where the DUT's
      requests go to the broadcast address.  This is the TFS's distinguishing requirement and it
      is met.
    - Sender is the DUT's vlan10 address 10.38.22.34 and its SVI MAC; target is the supplicant's
      address.  The supplicant answered all 5.
  STEP 2 VERDICT: PASS.

STEP 3 -- CONTROL: `type ping` MUST PRODUCE ICMP, NOT ARP.  Same supplicant, same interval,
          one word of configuration changed.

  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type ping
  awplus# show auth-web-server -> PingPolling: enabled / PingPollingType: Ping / PingInterval: 10

  tool: arpleg.py --tag L2-icmp-poll --iface eth3 --sniff-only --wait 45
  cap L2-icmp-poll-eth3.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
    x4  ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34  [00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x2  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.34 tell 10.38.22.33 [00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x2  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]

  Reading: the DUT now emits ICMP echo requests (type 8) at the same cadence, and **ZERO ARP
  requests of its own**.  The two ARP requests present go in the OPPOSITE direction -- source
  00:0d:b9:55:77:0e, the box refreshing its own cache for the DUT -- so they are not polls.
  `type arp` and `type ping` are therefore distinguishable on the wire, and step 2's ARP frames
  are attributable to the `type arp` setting rather than to polling in general.
  STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS.

STEP 4 -- DEAUTHENTICATION AFTER `failcount` UNANSWERED POLLS.  The TFS's purpose.

  awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type arp      (back to ARP)
  awplus# show auth-web-server   -> PingPollingType: ARP, FailCount: 3
  awplus# show auth supplicant   -> Supplicant name: ckpoll, portStatus: Authorized

  THE STIMULUS, DECLARED: the supplicant must stop answering its directed ARP polls while the
  LINK STAYS UP (a link-down would deauthenticate it for an unrelated reason and prove nothing).
  That was done with a host-NIC attribute on the box:

      sudo ip link set eth3 arp off        # eth3 then reads NOARP; link, carrier and the DUT's
                                           # port1.0.10 all stay up
      ... measurement ...
      sudo ip link set eth3 arp on         # reverted; NOARP count back to 0, verified

  This is an attribute of a lab NIC that exists only to drive this bench, it is the exact
  stimulus the feature is designed to detect, and it was reverted and verified in the same
  command. It is not a key, route, firewall rule, mount or device-trust change.

  tool: arpleg.py --tag L3-deauth --iface eth3 --sniff-only --wait 70
  cap L3-deauth-eth3.pcap: 7 frame(s)
    x3  ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
    x3  ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.41 tell 10.38.22.41  [00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x1  IP p2   0.0.0.0 -> 224.0.0.22                 [00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 -> 01:00:5e:00:00:16]

  Reading -- and this is the limb that must not rest on a supplicant count, so it does not:
    - The DUT sent **EXACTLY 3** directed ARP requests to 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e.  `failcount` is 3.
    - **NOT ONE ARP-REPLY from 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e appears** -- against 5 replies for 5 polls in
      step 2.  The polls genuinely went unanswered.
    - Polling then STOPPED: no 4th poll in the remaining ~40 s of the 70 s window.  The DUT gave
      up at failcount rather than continuing.
    - The other 4 frames are the third-party device (section 9), a gratuitous ARP for its own
      address and an IGMP report.  They are from a different MAC, carry different addresses, and
      are listed separately by the tool.  Neither is a poll and neither is answered by anything.

  THE DUT'S OWN LOG NAMES THE SUPPLICANT AND THE EVENT, by MAC:
    2026 Oct 15 06:35:41 802.1X[1061]: Web Authentication successful for
       ckpoll@000d.b955.770e(10.38.22.33) on port1.0.10
    2026 Oct 15 06:39:55 802.1X[1061]: port1.0.10: Supplicant ckpoll logoff, Mac 000d.b955.770e
    2026 Oct 15 06:39:56 802.1X[1061]: port1.0.10: Supplicant ckpoll unauthorized,
       Mac 000d.b955.770e

  The deauthenticated supplicant is named `ckpoll` and carries MAC `000d.b955.770e` -- the box's
  eth3 NIC, the one whose polls went unanswered.  The timing closes it: the NOARP window opened
  at about 06:38:45, three polls at `interval 10` with `timeout 1` ran out at about 06:39:55, and
  the logoff is stamped 06:39:55 with the unauthorized line one second later.
  Interface state afterwards:
    awplus# show auth supplicant      -> authorizedSupplicantNum: 0, Supplicant name: -
    awplus# show auth interface port1.0.10 -> portStatus: Unauthorized
  STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS -- and note the verdict rests on the three unanswered DIRECTED polls plus
  the DUT's own by-MAC log line, NOT on the supplicant count, which on this contended segment
