VERDICT: PASS -- "ARP Polling" on this platform is web-auth ping-polling (Project 2561), and it
works as the TFS specifies.  With a web-authenticated supplicant present and polling disabled,
the segment is silent for 40 s; with `auth-web-server ping-poll type arp` + `enable` and
`interval 10` the DUT emits ARP requests at that cadence addressed UNICAST to the supplicant's
own MAC rather than to the broadcast address, which is the TFS's distinguishing requirement;
`type ping` produces ICMP echo requests instead and no DUT-sourced ARP at all, so the ARP frames
are attributable to the type setting; when the supplicant stops answering, the DUT sends exactly
`failcount` (3) unanswered directed polls, stops, and deauthenticates it, with its own log naming
`Supplicant ckpoll logoff, Mac 000d.b955.770e`; the supplicant re-authenticates and polling
resumes; `no auth-web-server ping-poll enable` returns the segment to zero frames in 40 s; and
all four settings and the behaviour survive a reboot.  The TFS's proposed
`auth-web-server arp-poll enable` syntax does not exist on this build (`% Unrecognized command`)
and the shipped `auth-web-server ping-poll type arp` is the intended replacement per TFS revision
0.3 -- recorded as a documentation divergence, NOT graded UNSUPPORTED.
